        "text": "Americans got a tiny taste of Pakistani truck painting in the summer of 2002 at the Smithsonian Folklife Festival, when a truck painter and a bodywork expert brought a truck from Karachi to Washington, D.C. They decorated it right there on the National . theme, truck fancier Jonathan Mark, an anthropology professor at the University of Mall, as outdoor artists-in-residence. As a talent scout for the festival's Silk Road in incorporating the country's different styles of truck art. Their finished a Bedford, is now part of the Smithsonian's permanent collection. masterpiece, regions have evolved their own signature idioms. In Peshawar, trucks display far more Although Karachi is the country's principal center for truck decoration, other calligraphy than illustration. In Rawalpindi and Islamabad, designers cut out colored truck exterior. Artisans in Baluchistan and Peshawar are esteemed for their plastic sheets and layer them to create unusual patterns and geometric effects over the magnificently detailed woodwork carved on cabin doors and interiors. Camel-bone inlay is emblem of Sindh province, while stainless-steel peacock appliqués are popular both in Sindh and the Punjab.\n\nTruck art has developed over the decades. It was in the 1940s that trucks first began to deliver long-haul goods, and each company developed its own painted logo so that the mainly illiterate people could recognize them. After Partition in 1947, the trucks displayed solidarity with their young nation with, for example, the sign of one company featuring the geographical outlines of the new country. These logos became more and more flamboyant as trade became increasingly competitive. Truck decoration initially used the thousands of years old motifs from the camel caravans and ox-carts, but in the 1950s Haji Hussain effected enormous change. He had previously painted murals and frescoes in palaces in Gujarat, but when he settled in Karachi, he turned his skills to painting horse carriages and trucks with floral borders.\n\nIn the 1960's, Pakistan's economic boom included a great expansion of the transport industry. The British-built Bedford truck with its seven-foot panelled sides became the truck of choice because of its indestructibility, which enabled it to haul loads of up to twenty tons for twenty-five years and more. When Vauxhall stopped production of the Bedford trucks in the early Nineties, Japanese imports like Hino and Nissan supplanted them, but despite their superiority, drivers are nostalgic about the old Bedfords. As the owners' wealth increased during the 60s and 70s, and they jostled for business, truck decoration became more and more elaborate.\n\nNow in the twenty-first century, truck art has reached some kind of apogee, an explosion of brilliant colour, and executed with great skill and inventiveness. All arts including paintings is an embodimerit of an everlasting beauty. It gives a joy that is permanent and unfading and has an eternal impact on the human mind. Likewise, the art on the truck panels is static and unchanging and represents the world of art. When we look at such arts on our streets and roads, we pass from the world of transience to that of permanence for a while. Let us hope these traditions will be handed down to subsequent generations of artists. About the author: Rachel Redford has extensive experience in English language and literature teaching and assessments. She is the author of the secondary textbook series Oxford English: An international approach from the Oxford University Press UK. She has also written two Student Books and a Teacher's Guide for Oxford University Press to accompany the General Certificate of Secondary Education (GCSE) syllabus in the UK. Her other publications include Texts from Other Cultures, a collection of extracts from writings across the world. Rachel Redford also reviews audio books for British journals. She lives in Carisbrooke on the Isle of Wight in England. alteration(n) anthropology(n) apogee(n) appliqué(n) arabesque(n) artefact(n) blaring(adj.) boom(n) a change to something that makes it different the study of the human race, especially of its origins, development, customs and beliefs. the highest point of something, where it is greatest or most successful a type of needlework in which small pieces of cloth are sewn or stuck in a pattern onto a larger piece a type of design where lines wind around each other an object that is made by a person, especially something of historical or cultural interest to make a loud unpleasant noise to have a period of rapid growth chassis(n) dangling(adj.) deck(v) emblem(n) embodiment(n) fancier(n) fresco(n) fringe(n) from stem-to- stern(idiom) guild(n) inlays(n) in-residence(n) jostle(v) kaleidoscope(n) long-haul(n) magnate(n) medieval(adj.) motif(n) the frame that a vehicle is built on to hang or move freely; to hold something so that it hangs or moves freely to decorate somebody/something with something a design or picture that represents a country or an organization a person or thing that represents or is a typical example of an idea or a quality a person who has a special interest in something, especially somebody who keeps a particular type of bird or animal a picture that is painted on a wall while the plaster is still wet; the method of painting in this way the front part of somebody's hair that is cut so that it hangs over their forehead all the way from the front of a ship to the back an organization of people who do the same job or who have the same interests or aims to decorate the surface of something by putting pieces of wood or metal into it in such a way that the surface remains smooth Committed to live and work in a certain place, often for a specific length of time. to push roughly against somebody in a crowd. a toy consisting of a tube that you look through with loose pieces of coloured glass and mirrors at the end. When the tube is turned, the pieces of glass move and form different patterns involving the transport of goods or passengers over long distances a person who is rich, powerful and successful, especially in business connected with the Middle Ages (about AD 1000 to AD 1450) a design or a pattern used as a decoration Whatc mural(n) nostalgic(adj.) painstaking(adj.) propane(n) reckon(v) spiff up(v) supplant(v) talent scout(n) upholsterer(n) vibrant(adj.) of a painting) large and done on a wall, sometimes on an outside wall of a building having or bringing a sad feeling mixed with pleasure when you think of happy times in the past done with a lot of care, effort and attention to detail a flammable hydrocarbon gas of the alkane series, present in natural gas and used as bottled fuel. think something or have an opinion about something to make yourself/somebody/something look neat and attractive to take the place of somebody/something (especially somebody/something older or less modern) someone who looks for people who have the skills they want, especially in entertainment or sport person who covers a chair, etc. with soft material (=padding) and cloth or leather full of life and energy",
