        "text": "In a small part of the city West of Washington Square, the streets have gone wild. They turn in different directions. They are broken into small pieces called \"places.\" One street goes across itself one or two times. A painter once discovered something possible and valuable about this street. Suppose a painter had some painting materials for which he had not paid. Suppose he had no money. Suppose a man came to get the money. The man might walk down that street and suddenly meet himself coming back, without having received a cent!\n\nThis part of the city is called Greenwich Village. And to old Greenwich Village the painters soon came. Here they found rooms they like, with good light and at a low Sue and Johnsy lived at the top of a building with three floors. One of th young women came from Maine, the other from California. They had met at a restaur on Eighth Street. There they discovered that they liked the same kind of art, the sa kind of food, and the same kind of clothes./So they decided to live and work togeth موسم . .That was in the spring Toward winter a cold 'stranger entered Greenwich Village, No one could him, He walked around touching one person here and another there with his icy fing He was a bad sickness Doctors called him Pneumonia. On the east side of the city hurried, touching many people; but in the narrow streets of Greenwich Village he not move so quickly.\n\nMr. Pneumonia was not a nice old gentleman, A nice old gentleman would hurt a weak little woman from California. But Mr. Pneumonia touched Johnsy with cold fingers, She lay on her bed almost without moving, and she looked through window at the wall of the house next to hers: not hear.\n\nOne morning the busy doctor spoke to Sue alone in the hall, where Johnsy co \"She has a very small chance,\" he said. \"She has a chance, if she wants to If people don't want to live, I can't do much for them. Your little lady has decided she is not going to get well/ Is there something that is troubling her?\"\n\n\"She always wanted to go to Italy and paint a picture of the Bay of Naples,\" said Sue.\n\n\"Paint! Not paint. Is there anything worth being troubled about? A män?\" \"A man?\" said Sue. \"Is a man worth-No, doctor. There is not a man.\"",
