        "heading": "6. Me rubbed his eyes with his fists.",
        "text": "Me rubbed his eyes with his fists. \"Papa and mama say a man should never leave his post\".\n\n1 hugged him tightly and pressed my cheek against his. Then I rose, buttoned my coat, pulled my hat down firmly, left the cave and walked down the path. Following the road that had been blasted through the rock, I pushed on, the words of the child echoing in my head, \"A man should never leave his post\". A job was waiting me. I wanted to reach my destination without any further delay. About the Author Translated anonymously from the Chinese)\n\nAfter growing up in a poor family in the province of Shenshi (also spelled Shaanxi) TuPeng-cheng (born 1921) worked as a correspondent for the New China News during China's civil war in the late 1940's. His many notes about battles he witnessed, became important sources for his later fiction, including his acclaimed war novel, Defend Yenan, published in 1954.\n\nWinning praise throughout China for his depiction of military life, Tu enjoyed at flourishing career throughout the 1950s. In 1958 he published his next major novel In Peacetime, which describes workers constructing the Paoki to Chengtu (Baoji to Chengdu) Railway. Tu fell from favour in the 1960s when the government rewrote its history and condemned several former leaders whom Tu had praised in his fiction. His work was banned in China until 1978 when a thaw in the nation's totalitarian regime allowed Tu to resume his writing career. apparently(adv.)\n\nReadily seen or understood; evident, obvious crisscrossing(v) depot(n)\n\nTo move or cause to move in a crosswise pattern A storehouse or warehouse for supplies, usually the military ววววว -570 dusk(n) gale(n) gorge(n) hazy(adj.) imp(n) irritation(n) mutual(adj.) numb(v) pretty sharp ravine(n) rent(n) snowflakes(n)\n\nTwilight or the darker part of twilight; evening, sunset, nightfall A strong wind; a blast, storm A deep ravine, esp. one through which a river runs; canyon Characterised by reduced visibility; misty; foggy A small demon or devil; a mischievous child Something that irritates, bothers, annoys, enrages, provokes Experienced or expressed by each of two or more persons about the other; reciprocal; common or shared by two or more parties Deprived of feeling in limbs through cold; paralysed Quite intelligent A deep narrow steep-sided valley, esp. one formed by the action of running water; a gorge A slit or opening made by tearing; a tear or breach Delicate, fluffy mass of snow that falls as snow To turn or cause to turn in a twisting spinning fashion; spinning To fix or fasten with a clamp To present or represent as being worthy or regard; recommend; to praise; to express a good opinion of swirling(v) to clamp(v) to cock(v) To raise, perk up; to commend(v) to discern(v) to doze off(v) to flash(v) to gaze(v) to gleam(v) to haul(v)\n\nTo recognise or perceive clearly To fall into a light sleep To burst or cause to burst suddenly or intermittently into flame; to move very rapidly like a flash To look long and fixedly, in wonder or admiration; to stare A small beam or glow of light appearing momentarily; To drag or draw something with effort; to transport as in a truck Whatc366 to knock off(ph.v.) to obliterate(v) to offend(v) to peer(v) to scale(v) to scowl(v) to stamp(v) To finish work; To destroy every trace of, to wipe out completely To hurt the feelings, sense of dignity of someone To look intently with or as if with difficulty To climb to the top of a mountain by a ladder To contract the brows in a threatening or angry manner To put the foot down heavily on the ground To trip or fall while walking or running to stumble(v) undoubtedly (adv.) Without any doubt; vaguely(adv.)\n\nNot clearly; fuzzily, dimly",
